.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH kage 1  "kage 0.6.0" 
.SH NAME
kage \- A fast, hackable coding agent for your terminal, editor and browser
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBkage\fR [\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-print\fR] [\fB\-m\fR|\fB\-\-model\fR] [\fB\-\-system\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-session\fR] [\fB\-\-yolo\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no command and no \fB\-p\fR, kage opens its interactive TUI in the current directory. Type a prompt and press enter to send it. On an empty prompt, \fB?\fR shows the keys and a prompt starting with \fB/\fR runs a command. \fBctrl+q\fR quits.
.PP
With \fB\-p\fR, kage runs one prompt through the agent loop, streams the reply to stdout and exits.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-print\fR \fI<PRINT>\fR
Run a single prompt through the agent loop and stream the response to stdout
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-model\fR \fI<MODEL>\fR
Provider\-qualified model id (`provider/model`). Defaults to `[provider] default_model` when its provider has credentials, then the last model you used, then the first available provider\*(Aqs preferred model
.TP
\fB\-\-system\fR \fI<SYSTEM>\fR [default: You are kage, a helpful coding agent.]
System prompt to prepend
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-session\fR
Disable session recording. By default every run writes a JSONL session file under `$XDG_DATA_HOME/kage/sessions/<session\-id>.jsonl`
.TP
\fB\-\-yolo\fR
Start the session in allow mode: every tool call runs without asking, MCP servers included. A configured deny still denies. Same effect as `/permission allow`, applied at startup
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit one JSON object per event on stdout instead of plain text. Only meaningful with `\-p/\-\-print`. Each line carries the event\*(Aqs `type` (such as `text_delta`, `tool_call_start`, `message_appended`, or `run_ended`) plus the `session` it belongs to and a per\-session `seq` number, so external tools can parse the agent\*(Aqs output without screen\-scraping
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBkage list\fR
List recorded sessions in `$XDG_DATA_HOME/kage/sessions/`
.TP
\fBkage resume\fR
Resume a recorded session, appending new entries to the same file
.TP
\fIID\fR
Session id or unique prefix. Mutually exclusive with \-\-last
.TP
\fB\-\-last\fR
Resume the most recently created session of the current directory
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-print\fR\fI <PRINT>\fR
New user prompt to append in print mode. Without it the session opens in the interactive TUI
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-model\fR\fI <MODEL>\fR
Override the recorded model. Defaults to the model the session was last using
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit one JSON object per event on stdout instead of plain text. Same format as the top\-level `\-\-json` flag
.TP
\fBkage fork\fR
Fork a recorded session at a specific entry into a new session file
.TP
\fIID\fR
Source session id or unique prefix
.TP
\fB\-\-at\fR\fI <AT>\fR
Entry id (or unique prefix) to fork at; everything up through this entry is copied into the new session
.TP
\fBkage search\fR
Regex\-search recorded sessions
.TP
\fIQUERY\fR
Regex query, ripgrep\-style
.TP
\fBkage auth\fR
Manage saved provider API credentials
.TP
\fBkage auth login\fR
Save an API key for a provider, prompting for it without echo
.TP
\fIPROVIDER\fR
Provider id, or a media tool name (`generate_image`, `edit_image`, `generate_speech`) for the key that tool sends to its `base_url`. Omit to pick from a list
.TP
\fBkage auth logout\fR
Remove a saved API key
.TP
\fIPROVIDER\fR
Provider id
.TP
\fBkage auth list\fR
Show which known and custom providers have credentials available
.TP
\fBkage init\fR
First\-run setup wizard. Creates `~/.config/kage/config.toml`, scaffolds the data directories, and offers to save a provider API key. Idempotent: rerunning without `\-\-force` keeps any existing config in place
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Overwrite an existing `~/.config/kage/config.toml` instead of keeping it
.TP
\fB\-\-non\-interactive\fR
Skip every prompt. Useful for scripted bootstrap; will still write a fresh config when one is missing but never asks for an API key
.TP
\fBkage doctor\fR
Diagnose the kage install: parses config, lists available providers, validates plugins, probes each MCP server. Exit code is `0` when no check fails, `1` otherwise
.TP
\fBkage providers\fR
List the provider ids this kage knows: the builtins and the catalog, with the wire protocol each speaks and whether `[providers.<id>]` may override it or `[providers.custom.<id>]` may take its id. Custom providers from the config are not listed
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print one JSON object, `{"providers": [...]}`, whose entries carry `id`, `api`, `override` and `custom`
.TP
\fBkage completions\fR
Print a shell completion script for `kage` to stdout
.TP
\fISHELL\fR
Target shell
.TP
\fBkage rpc\fR
Agent Client Protocol server: speak JSON\-RPC over stdio so an editor (Zed, Neovim, ...) can drive kage. Messages are newline\-delimited JSON. Each request is answered, and loop progress is streamed back as ACP `session/update` notifications
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-model\fR\fI <MODEL>\fR
Provider\-qualified model id (`provider/model`). Defaults the same way as the top\-level `\-m`
.TP
\fB\-\-system\fR\fI <SYSTEM>\fR
System\-prompt role override forwarded to the agent loop
.TP
\fB\-\-system\-file\fR\fI <PATH>\fR
A file whose contents are appended to the system prompt
.TP
\fB\-\-yolo\fR
Start every session in allow mode: every tool call runs without asking, MCP servers included. A configured deny still denies, and a client may still change the mode
.TP
\fBkage serve\fR
Serve the Agent Client Protocol over WebSocket so remote editors and ACP clients can drive kage over the network. One endpoint (`/acp`) behind a bearer token; every connection shares one engine. The same listener serves the web client bundle at `/`, so a browser needs only the page URL and the token. The startup output on stderr carries the connect URL, the only place the token is ever printed
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-model\fR\fI <MODEL>\fR
Provider\-qualified model id (`provider/model`). Defaults the same way as the top\-level `\-m`
.TP
\fB\-\-system\fR\fI <SYSTEM>\fR
System\-prompt role override forwarded to the agent loop
.TP
\fB\-\-host\fR\fI <HOST>\fR
Address to bind. Loopback by default; a non\-loopback address prints a warning because kage itself has no TLS
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR\fI <PORT>\fR
TCP port to bind. `0` picks a free port and the connect URL shows it
.TP
\fB\-\-rotate\-token\fR
Replace the stored token with a fresh one before serving, so every client holding the old connect URL must be sent the new one
.TP
\fB\-\-web\-dir\fR\fI <WEB_DIR>\fR
Directory with the web client bundle to serve at `/` (built from `crates/kage\-desktop`). Defaults to `$XDG_DATA_HOME/kage/web`. A missing or empty directory only disables the web UI; `/acp` keeps working
.TP
\fB\-\-fetch\-web\fR
Download the web client bundle matching this kage into the web directory, replacing what is there, then serve
.TP
\fBkage trust\fR
Trust the current directory\*(Aqs `.kage/config.toml`. Until a project is trusted, its `mcp`, `permissions`, `plugins.capabilities` and `tools` settings are ignored. Trust covers the values as they are now, so editing any of them asks again
.TP
\fB\-\-revoke\fR
Forget the trust recorded for this directory
.TP
\fBkage models\fR
Manage the model catalog. kage ships a snapshot of the curated catalog; `kage models refresh` fetches a newer one into the model cache, which later runs use in place of the snapshot\*(Aqs model entries
.TP
\fBkage models refresh\fR
Download the curated model catalog into `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/kage/models.json`, and with a `ChatGPT` sign\-in the plan\*(Aqs models into `chatgpt\-models.json` beside it. It can add models and update their metadata, never provider endpoints or credentials. Only runs when asked; kage never refreshes on its own
.TP
\fB\-\-source\fR\fI <SOURCE>\fR
Read the catalog from here instead of the published feed. A `file://` URL reads a local file, which is how an offline run or a test pins a catalog. The `KAGE_CATALOG_URL` environment variable sets the same thing
.TP
\fBkage models fetch\fR
List the models a provider endpoint serves. Names a saved provider to test its saved settings; the flags fill what the config leaves out, or probe an endpoint that is not configured
.TP
\fIPROVIDER\fR
Provider id: a `[providers.<id>]` entry, an override, or a builtin such as `openai` or `anthropic`
.TP
\fB\-\-base\-url\fR\fI <BASE_URL>\fR
Endpoint base URL, when the provider has none saved
.TP
\fB\-\-kind\fR\fI <KIND>\fR
Wire protocol: openai, anthropic or gemini. Guessed from the provider when omitted
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-key\fR\fI <API_KEY>\fR
API key, used for this request only
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-key\-env\fR\fI <API_KEY_ENV>\fR
Environment variable that holds the API key
.TP
\fB\-\-models\-url\fR\fI <MODELS_URL>\fR
The URL the model list is read from, when the endpoint\*(Aqs own will not do \- a models.dev\-style directory document, say. Overrides the provider\*(Aqs saved models URL. A key and headers are only sent when it shares the base URL\*(Aqs host and port
.TP
\fB\-\-save\fR
Save the fetched models into the saved `[providers.custom.<provider>]` entry: ids it does not list yet are appended as fresh `[[...models]]` tables, listed ones and their comments stay untouched. Needs the entry to exist; registered providers keep their models in the catalog instead
.TP
\fBkage plugin\fR
Manage plugins: list them, install and update packages from the plugin registry (kura unless `[plugins] registry` names another), grant capabilities, and turn plugins on or off
.TP
\fBkage plugin list\fR
List installed plugins: kind, version, where each came from, and whether it is disabled, edited or has an update
.TP
\fBkage plugin search\fR
Search the registry by name, description and tags. Without a query, list every package
.TP
\fIQUERY\fR
Words that must all match
.TP
\fB\-\-refresh\fR
Fetch the index again instead of using the cached one
.TP
\fBkage plugin info\fR
Show a registry package: its releases, capabilities and links
.TP
\fINAME\fR
Package name
.TP
\fB\-\-refresh\fR
Fetch the index again instead of using the cached one
.TP
\fBkage plugin install\fR
Install a package from the registry, checking every file against the index. Lists the capabilities it asks for and offers to grant them
.TP
\fIPACKAGE\fR
`name`, or `name@version` for a given release
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Replace a script or local package of the same name, or discard local changes to an installed one
.TP
\fB\-\-grant\fR
Grant what the package asks for without asking
.TP
\fBkage plugin update\fR
Update packages installed from the registry to their newest release this kage can load
.TP
\fINAMES\fR
Packages to update; every one when none is named
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Discard local changes to a package being updated
.TP
\fB\-\-grant\fR
Grant newly asked\-for capabilities without asking
.TP
\fBkage plugin remove\fR
Remove a plugin, script or package. A linked package loses only its link
.TP
\fINAME\fR
Plugin name
.TP
\fBkage plugin grant\fR
Grant a plugin capabilities in `[plugins.capabilities]`. Without names, grants everything a package\*(Aqs plugin.toml declares
.TP
\fINAME\fR
Plugin name
.TP
\fICAPABILITIES\fR
Capabilities to grant
.TP
\fBkage plugin revoke\fR
Take capabilities back from a plugin; all of them without names
.TP
\fINAME\fR
Plugin name
.TP
\fICAPABILITIES\fR
Capabilities to revoke
.TP
\fBkage plugin enable\fR
Let a disabled plugin load again
.TP
\fINAME\fR
Plugin name
.TP
\fBkage plugin disable\fR
Keep a plugin from loading without removing it, through `[plugins] disabled`
.TP
\fINAME\fR
Plugin name
.TP
\fBkage mcp\fR
Model Context Protocol: expose kage\*(Aqs built\-in tools to another agent over stdio (newline\-delimited JSON\-RPC) with `kage mcp serve`, or log in to remote MCP servers that need OAuth
.TP
\fBkage mcp serve\fR
Serve kage\*(Aqs built\-in tools as an MCP server over stdio. Calls are checked against `[permissions]`, and a tool whose verdict is `ask` is refused because there is no one to ask
.TP
\fB\-\-tools\fR\fI <TOOLS>\fR
Comma\-separated built\-in tools to expose. Unknown names are an error
.TP
\fBkage mcp login\fR
Log in to a remote MCP server with OAuth. Prints the authorization URL, opens it in a browser when one is available, and waits for the browser to return, or for the redirected URL to be pasted on a machine without one. The token is stored in `$XDG_DATA_HOME/kage/mcp\-auth.json` (mode 0600)
.TP
\fISERVER\fR
Server name from `[mcp.servers.<name>]`
.TP
\fBkage mcp logout\fR
Forget the stored OAuth token of a remote MCP server
.TP
\fISERVER\fR
Server name from `[mcp.servers.<name>]`
.PP
Run \fBkage \fR\fIcommand\fR\fB \-\-help\fR for the options of a command.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI~/.config/kage/config.toml\fR
User config, written by kage init.
.TP
\fI~/.config/kage/init.lua\fR
Lua config the TUI runs after config.toml.
.TP
\fI<workdir>/.kage/config.toml\fR
Project config, merged over the user config. Its mcp, permissions, plugins.capabilities and tools tables apply only after kage trust.
.TP
\fI~/.config/kage/{plugins,themes,agents,skills,templates}/\fR
Lua plugins, user themes, agent definitions, skills and prompt templates.
.TP
\fI~/.local/share/kage/sessions/\fR
Recorded sessions, one JSONL file each.
.TP
\fI~/.local/share/kage/auth.json\fR
Saved provider credentials (mode 0600).
.TP
\fI~/.local/state/kage/\fR
Session state, input history and trusted projects (trust.json).
.TP
\fI~/.cache/kage/models.json\fR
Model catalog written by kage models refresh.
.TP
\fI~/.cache/kage/chatgpt\-models.json\fR
ChatGPT plan models, written at sign\-in and by kage models refresh.
.PP
XDG_CONFIG_HOME, XDG_DATA_HOME, XDG_STATE_HOME and XDG_CACHE_HOME replace the ~/.config, ~/.local/share, ~/.local/state and ~/.cache roots.
